course: dictionary
lesson: 9520
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some small words have more than one meaning. The word still is one of them, so you need its context to know which meaning fits. Context means the words and sentences around it.
explain: «Chloe and Bella are still frenemies in Year 6.» Still means up to now, or even now. Try swapping in «up to now» and the sentence still makes sense.
explain: «The potoo sits still on a branch looking exactly like a dead stump.» Here still means not moving. «Older children often help a lot, but they still need breakfast, sleep and time for themselves» uses still to mean even so, in spite of helping.
explain: Ask yourself: which meaning fits this sentence? Then test it by putting that meaning in place of still and reading the sentence again.
fact: Still means up to now, or even now: «still frenemies» means they are frenemies up to now.
fact: Still means not moving or calm: «sit still» means do not move.
fact: Still means even so, in spite of that: «they still need breakfast» means they need it all the same.
fact: Still can make a comparison stronger: «still better» means even better.
fact: Still water has no bubbles, and still drinks are not fizzy.
exercise: «The potoo sits still on a branch.» What does still mean here? | Even now | Full of bubbles | Very quickly | *Not moving
exercise: «The river Lybid and two hills still carry their names.» What does still mean? | Even so, in spite of that | Much faster | *Even now, up to this time | Not moving
exercise: Ella lost the race. Her friend says, «Good try, still.» What does still mean? | Up to now | *Anyway, even so | Not moving | Stronger, even better
test: «The lake was so still that it reflected the moon.» What does still mean? | Up to now | Even so | *Calm and not moving | Full of bubbles
test: «The second story was still better than the first.» What does still mean here? | *Even better, making the comparison stronger | Up to now | In spite of that | Not moving
exam: Which meaning of still fits best in «Chloe and Bella are still frenemies in Year 6»? | *Up to now, even at this time | Even so, anyway | Not moving | Without bubbles
